/* ⚠ NINTH SLOT: MOVEMENT PATTERN. `region` (slot 8) says WHICH TISSUE a lift loads — wrist_flexors,
   rear_delts — and it already exists because the v574-v577 wrist work needed it. `pattern` says WHICH
   MOVEMENT, and three separate problems all turned out to need it and none could be solved without it:
     - duplicate detection: two curls on one day is redundancy, but Seated + Standing Calf Raise is
       soleus vs gastrocnemius and RDL + Lying Leg Curl is hip-hinge vs knee-flexion. A rule built on
       muscle coverage alone flags all three, and measured 456 "redundant" slots across 186 days that
       way — roughly 2.5 per day, an unknown share of them legitimate variation.
     - delt-head balance: three lateral raises stacked while rear delts sit under MEV.
     - forearm direction: the v574 problem, only ever solved for forearms because only forearms had
       a region tag to solve it with.
   Kept as a SEPARATE slot rather than folded into `region` because a lift has both independently:
   a Standing Calf Raise is region `gastrocnemius`, pattern `plantarflexion-straight-knee`. Untagged
   is legal and means unknown — an absent pattern must never read as "matches everything", which is
   how a coverage rule turns into a false positive. */
/* The tags themselves, keyed by id rather than added as a ninth tuple element, so a review diff shows
   the pattern change alone rather than 353 touched rows. Generated by explicit rule with NO
   fallbacks — the twelve exercises no rule matched were surfaced and decided by hand rather than
   absorbed. The first attempt DID have per-part fallbacks, reported "353 of 353 tagged", and hid a
   dozen wrong tags behind that perfect score: Leg Press and Hack Squat Calf Raise read as
   straight-knee (they are bent-knee, soleus-biased — the exact distinction this field exists for),
   Sissy and Cyclist Squat read as `squat` rather than knee-extension, and both Tibialis Raises read
   as knee-extension rather than dorsiflexion. A tagger that cannot report "I do not know" will
   always report certainty. */
